mary\": \"Serious adverse reactions associated with SSRIs include serotonin syndrome and increased suicide risk\",\n            \"explanation\": \"SSRIs are linked to potentially life-threatening adverse effects, most notably serotonin syndrome and elevated suicide risk. Serotonin syndrome—a condition resulting from excessive serotonergic activity—has been reported in association with SSRI use and has led to fatalities in documented cases [Data: Relationships (7876, 16407)]. Additionally, SSRIs carry a recognized risk of inducing suicidal thoughts or behaviors, particularly in vulnerable populations [Data: Relationships (7878)]. These findings highlight critical safety considerations that impact prescribing practices and regulatory guidance, especially in pediatric and adolescent patients.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"SSRIs are contraindicated or used cautiously in pediatric depression due to insufficient evidence and safety concerns\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Despite their widespread use in adults, SSRIs are not recommended as first-line treatment for childhood depression. Guidelines suggest psychological interventions should be prioritized, given the lack of sufficient randomized controlled trials supporting SSRI efficacy in mild-to-moderate pediatric depression and concerns about adverse effects like emotional blunting and suicidality [Data: Entities (277); Relationships (7374)]. This caution reflects broader regulatory and clinical wariness regarding antidepressant use in younger populations, contributing to the community’s risk profile.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Multiple systemic adverse effects are documented for SSRIs, including hematologic and reproductive impacts\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Beyond neuropsychiatric risks, SSRIs are associated with systemic adverse reactions. These include blood system abnormalities—such as leukopenia, thrombocytopenia, and aplastic anemia—as well as reproductive system disorders, including sexual dysfunction and priapism in males [Data: Entities (17353, 16475); Relationships (16402, 16407)]. One case report describes a 58-year-old male who developed abnormal penile erection after accidental overdose of citalopram, requiring surgical intervention [Data: Relationships (16402)]. These effects underscore the multi-organ impact of SSRIs and the importance of monitoring beyond psychiatric outcomes.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Drug–drug interactions pose significant clinical risks, especially with CYP450-metabolized agents\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The metabolism of SSRIs via CYP450 enzymes creates substantial interaction risks with other medications. For example, paroxetine inhibits CYP2D6, which can elevate blood concentrations of co-administered drugs like aripiprazole, necessitating dose adjustments [Data: Entities (9136, 5386); Relationships (4749, 2000)]. Similarly, St. John’s Wort extract (圣约翰草提取物), though herbal, induces CYP3A4 and can reduce cyclosporine levels in transplant patients, risking graft rejection [Data: Entities (12389); Relationships (43811)]. These interactions demonstrate how even non-prescription or structurally distinct agents within this community can critically affect therapeutic outcomes.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Visual and neurological side effects are recurrent across multiple drugs in the community\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Blurred vision is a documented adverse reaction not only for SSRIs indirectly but also for other medications in the network, such as tolterodine (酒石酸托特罗定) and its extended-release formulation, both of which explicitly list vision blurring as a possible side effect [Data: Entities (18506, 8742, 8743); Relationships (16449, 16450)]. Additionally, tremor appears as a symptom of serotonin syndrome and is linked to serotonergic overactivity [Data: Entities (18561); Relationships (7655)]. These sensory and motor disturbances reflect the broad neurological impact of agents in this pharmacological community.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Specific antidepressants show targeted indications but also off-label or complex usage patterns\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Paroxetine, for instance, is indicated for tic disorders (抽动障碍), suggesting a role beyond standard depressive or anxiety conditions [Data: Entities (1188, 5386); Relationships (21381)]. However, its use may be complicated by cardiac effects like QT prolongation, leading clinicians to discontinue it in favor of alternatives like aripiprazole and duloxetine in certain cases [Data: Relationships (21381)]. This illustrates the nuanced clinical decision-making required when managing patients within this drug community, balancing efficacy, indication, and safety.\"\n        }\n    ],\n    \"rating\": 7.5,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The community poses a high impact due to the serious and sometimes fatal adverse effects of SSRIs, significant drug interaction risks affecting multiple organ systems, and cautious use recommendations in vulnerable populations like children.\"\n}", "refusal": null, "role": "assistant", "annotations": null, "audio": null, "function_call": null, "tool_calls": null, "provider_specific_fields": {"refusal": null}}, "provider_specific_fields": {}}], "created": 1782126740, "model": "qwen3-max", "object": "chat.completion", "moderation": null, "service_tier": null, "system_fingerprint": null, "usage": {"completion_tokens": 1486, "prompt_tokens": 7138, "total_tokens": 8624, "completion_tokens_details": null, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": null, "cached_tokens": 1280}}, "formatted_response": null, "content": "{\n    \"title\": \"SSRI and Related Antidepressant Drug Community\",\n    \"summary\": \"This community centers on Selective Serotonin Reuptake Inhibitors (SSRIs) and related antidepressant drugs, including specific agents like paroxetine, fluvoxamine, and St